From: gaokun-android contributors <nekovahiru@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: qcom: sc8280xp: retune the WSA speaker gain ceilings for gaokun3

NOT FOR UPSTREAM -- this raises a limit upstream put in deliberately. It is
here because on this machine the limit is in the wrong place, not because
the concern behind it is wrong.

Upstream caps both speaker gain stages in one go:

	/*
	 * Set limit of -3 dB on Digital Volume and 0 dB on PA Volume
	 * to reduce the risk of speaker damage until we have active
	 * speaker protection in place.
	 */
	snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WSA_RX0 Digital Volume", 81);
	snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "SpkrLeft PA Volume", 17);

The two stages are not equivalent, and an earlier revision of this patch
picked the wrong one. It raised the digital ceiling to 90 (+6 dB) and left
the PA ceiling alone, on the strength of a measurement that reported PA
Volume as having no effect on output. Re-measured, that is wrong on both
counts.

Measured on the device: 440 Hz tone played through tinyplay on the speaker
PCM, recorded on the built-in mic, Goertzel at the recovered fundamental.
All rows below are from one uninterrupted run, so they are comparable to
each other; runs minutes apart are not (see the caveat at the end).

Source at -6 dBFS, a realistic loud-content level:

  WSA_RX digital   PA Volume     level        THD
       81              12      -25.38 dBFS   -45.21 dB
       84              12      -22.20        -33.11
       81              17      -22.29        -34.33
       84              17      -19.40        -39.81
       90              12      -17.28        -20.08   <- what we ship today
       90              17      -14.16        -20.78

Source at -20 dBFS, the same three stages, as a control:

       81              12      -39.41 dBFS   -57.56 dB
       84              17      -33.40        -54.69
       90              12      -30.34        -52.82

Two things fall out of this.

First, the digital ceiling is the wrong lever. At -20 dBFS, stepping the
digital control 81 -> 90 delivers +9.07 dB for a nominal +9 dB and THD stays
below -52 dB: the stage is linear and the compander is not eating anything.
At -6 dBFS the same step delivers +8.10 dB and THD collapses from -45.2 dB
to -20.1 dB. That is clipping, and it is just arithmetic: the control scale
is value - 84 dB, so 90 is +6 dB, and -6 dBFS content plus 6 dB of gain is
0 dBFS. The configuration we ship today therefore runs about 10% distortion
on any content that approaches -6 dBFS. An earlier note in this tree
attributed the missing gain to the WSA883x compander; the -20 dBFS control
row rules that out.

So the digital ceiling is set to 84 rather than 90. 84 is exactly unity, and
it is also the driver's own reset value -- upstream's 81 actively pulls the
path 3 dB below unity. Capping at unity means no mixer setting can clip the
digital path, which is a property worth having and not just a number.

Second, PA Volume does work; the earlier measurement sampled it entirely
inside a flat region of its scale. The pa_gain TLV is

	 0..14  = -3.00 dB (flat)
	15..29  = -3.00 + (n - 15) * 1.50 dB
	30..31  = +18.00 dB

so the values that were compared -- 12, 6 and 1 -- are all the same -3.00 dB
step, and no difference between them was ever possible. Stepping across the
knee instead:

	PA Volume  13      14      15      16      17
	level    -25.19  -25.15  -25.24  -23.69  -22.26  dBFS

13/14/15 agree to within 0.09 dB and then each step adds 1.55 and 1.43 dB,
against the 1.50 dB the TLV specifies. The scale is real and measured, which
is what makes extrapolating past the cap defensible: 21 is +6 dB and 23
is +9 dB.

Being post-DAC, PA gain costs no digital headroom, which is why 84/17 is
both quieter and 19.7 dB cleaner than 90/12. Raise the PA ceiling to 23 and
let userspace pick a level under it.

Also re-measured, because both were recorded here as fact and both are
wrong: the BOOST port switch moves the output by +0.07 dB (archived run)
and -0.28 dB (fresh run), i.e. nothing. It is a SoundWire port enable, not the
amplifier's boost converter, so a later claim of "+4.1 dB" for it is not
reproducible. The compander switch likewise does not change level.

Risk accepted, and what is not known: these are the speakers of a fanless
12.35" tablet with no factory calibration (it lived in the Windows install,
which is gone), so the amp's protection has nothing to calibrate against. A
ceiling of +9 dB is half of the +18 dB the part itself allows. Two things
argue for not going further without more work: both WSA883x instances sit in
SoundWire "Alert" with unserviced interrupts, and identical mixer settings
measured minutes apart drift by up to 7.5 dB (0.57 dB within a single run),
which means there is an uncontrolled gain variable in this path that nobody
has explained yet. Raising a ceiling is safe; raising the level actually
written to it should follow a listening check and a look at
/sys/class/hwmon/*/temp1_input.

---
--- a/sound/soc/qcom/sc8280xp.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/sc8280xp.c
@@ -39,12 +39,26 @@ static int sc8280xp_snd_init(struct snd_soc_pcm_runtime *rtd)
 	case WSA_CODEC_DMA_RX_0:
 	case WSA_CODEC_DMA_RX_1:
 		/*
-		 * Set limit of -3 dB on Digital Volume and 0 dB on PA Volume
-		 * to reduce the risk of speaker damage until we have active
-		 * speaker protection in place.
+		 * Upstream caps this at -3 dB digital and 0 dB PA "to reduce
+		 * the risk of speaker damage until we have active speaker
+		 * protection in place". Retuned for this machine, where the
+		 * two stages were measured rather than assumed:
+		 *
+		 * Digital is capped at unity (84) instead of -3 dB (81). The
+		 * scale is value - 84 dB, so anything above 84 borrows from
+		 * digital headroom -- at +6 dB, -6 dBFS content lands exactly
+		 * on 0 dBFS and THD measures -20 dB. Unity is the highest
+		 * setting that cannot clip.
+		 *
+		 * PA is capped at +9 dB (23) instead of 0 dB (17). It is
+		 * post-DAC, so it costs no digital headroom, and it is the
+		 * only stage that can add level without clipping. Its TLV is
+		 * flat at -3 dB over 0..14 and then 1.50 dB per step; that was
+		 * confirmed on hardware (15 -> 16 -> 17 measured +1.55 and
+		 * +1.43 dB), so 21 is +6 dB and 23 is +9 dB.
 		 */
-		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WSA_RX0 Digital Volume", 81);
-		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WSA_RX1 Digital Volume", 81);
-		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "SpkrLeft PA Volume", 17);
-		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "SpkrRight PA Volume", 17);
+		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WSA_RX0 Digital Volume", 84);
+		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "WSA_RX1 Digital Volume", 84);
+		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "SpkrLeft PA Volume", 23);
+		snd_soc_limit_volume(card, "SpkrRight PA Volume", 23);
 		break;
